{
 "note": "R98b - X360 NHL 09 `.ast` packet headers: why the R91 XMA2 packet reading gives negative first-block deltas (-3712), inexact middle\nblocks (942 / 946 streams) and an undetected `packet_frame_count_0` control on NHL 09 alone (R98). Discriminating test between two\npublic packet-header layouts (Microsoft XMA documentation as read by the ffmpeg / vgmstream readers; not vendored - the layouts are\nrestated here as bit positions only):\n  XMA2 packet header (32 bits): FrameCount 6 | FrameOffsetInBits 15 | PacketMetadata 3 | PacketSkipCount 8\n  XMA1 packet header (32 bits): SequenceNumber 4 | Unused 2 | FrameOffsetInBits 15 | PacketSkipCount 11\nBoth keep FrameOffsetInBits at bits 25..11. Under XMA1 the top 4 bits must count 0..15 cyclically along a stream's packets and bits\n27..26 must be 0; under XMA2 the top 6 bits are a frame count and bits 10..8 a metadata value (1 on every NHL 10-Legacy packet, R84 /\nR91). Independent of either header, the frames inside a packet can be walked: each XMA frame starts with a 15-bit length (in bits,\nincluding the field); walking from FrameOffsetInBits until a 0x7FFF terminator / packet end counts the frames STARTING in the packet;\n\u03a3 (frames x 512) per block is then compared with the block's samples word - the sample rule that R91 established on NHL 10-Legacy\nthrough the header count, re-derived here without trusting the header.\nInputs: X360 NHL 09 loose `audio/sfx/{crowds,playercalls,sfx}.abk/.ast` (byte-identical to the `data0.big` / `audioboot.big` members,\nR98 hashes) and X360 NHL 10 loose as the control (read-only). Usage: python r98b_x360_09_packet_header.py <out.json>\n",
